#!/usr/bin/env rexx
/*
   name:    1-110_messageDigest.rxj
   invoke:  "rexx 1-110_messageDigest.rxj"
            ... Rexx script to demonstrate creating a digest from arbitrary data
                using the SHA-256 algorithm in this case (search with "javadoc
                MessageDigest" to see additional algorithms one can use);
                it demonstrates turning Rexx strings into Java byte arrays
                and Java byte arrays into Rexx strings using the external function
                BsfRawBytes()

            "rexxj2 1-110_messageDigest.rxj"      (Windows)
            "rexxj2.sh 1-110_messageDigest"   (Unix)
             ... Java will load Rexx and execute the Rexx script, which uses Java classes

   purpose: this program demonstrates the external function BsfRawBytes() to turn a
            Rexx string into a Java byte array, a Java byte array into a Rexx string
            which makes it easy to take advantage of Java methods that expect or return
            Java byte arrays as is the case with some methods in the Java class
            java.security.MessageDigest

   date:    2022-01-31

   changed: --

   author:  Rony G. Flatscher

   last change: $Revision: $ $Author: $ $Date: $

   license(s): see end of this file
*/

clz       = bsf.loadClass("java.security.MessageDigest") -- load the Java class
md        = clz~getInstance("SHA-256") -- get instance for the SHA-256 algorithm
data      = "Hello, my beloved world." -- data to be digested
arrData   = BsfRawBytes(data)          -- turn Rexx string into a Java byte array
arrDigest = md~digest(arrData)         -- returns digest as a Java byte array
digest    = BsfRawBytes(arrDigest)     -- turn Java byte array into a Rexx string
say "data        :" pp(data)           -- show digested data
say "digest (raw):" pp(digest)         -- show raw digest
say "digest (hex):" digest~c2x         -- show digest as hex

::requires BSF.CLS      -- get Java bridge

